The test asserts `check['uses']` is null AND `steps` hasLength(1) — i.e., the test PINS the missing checkout as a feature ("must not even pay a checkout"). But the step's `run:` references a repo file `scripts/release_dedupe_check.sh` — impossible without checkout. The test was written to enforce the broken design. This makes the regression guard actively wrong: it guarantees the job will keep failing on the runner. That strengthens BLOCKING #1: not only does the workflow fail, the guard test locks the failure in. The fix must EITHER inline the script (like release-pending) and adjust the test to pin the inline logic, OR add a checkout step and update the test (`steps` hasLength(2), uses checkout pinned SHA).

Also the test pins "any status dedupes" (`isNot(contains('--status'))`) — locking in BLOCKING #2 (failed push run → dedupe). The test reason even repeats the wrong concurrency claim: "in the hot path the push twin is still in_progress when the release event fires" — as analyzed, by the time the script RUNS (release twin dequed after push twin finished), the push twin is completed with a terminal conclusion. Actually wait — let me double-check this claim carefully because it's central to finding #2.

Concurrency: `group: ci-${{ github.ref }}`. Push run for tag v1.0.580 → group ci-refs/tags/v1.0.580. Release run same ref → same group. cancel-in-progress = event==pull_request → false. So the release run is "Pending" until the push run's jobs ALL finish (concurrency applies at run level: only one run in the group is "in progress"; the release run waits). The release-dedupe job therefore STARTS only after the push run finished entirely. At that moment `gh run list` sees the push run with status=completed and conclusion success/failure/cancelled. So "any status — in the hot path the push twin is still in_progress" is wrong; the truth is "any CONCLUSION — including failure/cancelled". The in_progress case can only happen if the push run hasn't started or is somehow still running when the release run deques — impossible within the same group... unless the release run was created BEFORE the push run? No: tag push precedes release creation in every flow (auto-release creates tag first). Edge: manual release creation on a tag pushed seconds ago while the push run is queued behind ANOTHER run? The group is per-ref, unique per tag, so no other run occupies it. The push run starts immediately. So in every realistic path, the push run is COMPLETED when the dedupe check executes. The "any status" design decision is thus strictly worse than checking conclusion — it skips the gate exactly when the gate failed.

Let me also consider: could the release event arrive while the push run is in progress AND the release run NOT be blocked by concurrency? Only if the group differs — e.g., `github.ref` differs between events? For a release event, github.ref = refs/tags/<tag>. For push of the tag, refs/tags/<tag>. Same. OK.

So BLOCKING #2 stands: on a failed/cancelled push gate, the release twin attaches publishable binaries + signs them + installer-smoke with no green gate. Old behavior: full gate re-ran → red → no binaries. Regression: fail-open.

Fix suggestion: fetch `conclusion` (or status) alongside createdAt; dedupe only if the run's conclusion == 'success' OR status in (queued, in_progress) (in-progress = gate still running — the release twin skipping the gate is safe only if... hmm, if in_progress and it later FAILS, same hole. Truly safe: dedupe only on conclusion == 'success'. If the push twin is still running (shouldn't happen due to concurrency, but belt), dedupe=false → full gate → the twin re-runs the gate (old behavior, redundant but safe). At minimum: exclude failure/cancelled/startup_failure/timed_out conclusions.)

And `binaries`' dedupe arm: same issue at a second layer — by binaries execution time the push twin is done; the arm `dedupe == 'true'` trusts the earlier decision. If the check is fixed to dedupe only on success, the binaries arm inherits safety. Fine.

